# sha256 of each prebuilt libmonero_wallet2_api_c, by platform directory.
#
# This file is the trust anchor for ./fetch-libs.sh. The libraries themselves
# are gitignored and distributed as release assets, which are mutable — anyone
# with write access can replace an asset at the same URL and nothing in git
# changes. These hashes are in git, so a swap shows up as a diff on a commit
# instead of as a silently different binary linked into a wallet.
#
# The hash is of the UNCOMPRESSED library, the artifact that actually gets
# linked. That keeps it stable if the assets are ever recompressed, and the
# .tar.xz carries its own integrity check for transport.
#
# Written by ./publish-libs.sh. Verify a rebuild against it with:
#     cd lib && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS      # shasum -a 256 -c on macOS
#
# PLACEHOLDER — NOTHING IS PUBLISHED YET.
#
# The hashes below are real: all ten came from one run of the Dockerfile in
# this directory, at the monero_c pin below, and each library was checked for
# the right architecture and for the exported
# MONERO_Wallet_setAllowMismatchedDaemonVersion that the cgo bindings need.
# `sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS` from lib/ passes against a tree built that way.
#
# The Android platforms are also short a line each. They ship libc++_shared.so
# beside the wallet library -- monero_c links libc++ dynamically, and
# wallet/android/bind.sh wants the NDK copy the library was built with -- but
# those three files were not kept from the run these hashes came from, so there
# is nothing honest to write for them here. ./publish-libs.sh hashes whatever it
# packages, so they appear when the Android platforms are published; until then
# a bare run skips those platforms with a warning rather than publishing them
# without the file, and naming one on its command line is an error.
#
# What is missing is the release. No assets have been uploaded, so this file
# is not yet an anchor for anything a third party can fetch. Note the absence
# of a "# Release:" line: fetch-libs.sh reads the tag from that line, so
# without it the script stops with an explicit message rather than downloading
# from a release that has no assets. Do not add one by hand — ./publish-libs.sh
# writes it, together with the hashes of exactly what it uploaded, so the tag
# and the hashes always describe the same release.
#
# So this commit says "these are the bytes this tree produces", not "these are
# the bytes you will be served". Treat the commit where publish-libs.sh rewrites
# this file as the point where the anchor starts meaning something.
#
# Reproducibility is still unestablished. One data point so far:
# x86_64-linux-android was built twice on the same machine, an hour apart and
# with a different patch set applied, and produced identical bytes. That rules
# out timestamps or build paths leaking into the output. It says nothing about
# whether a different machine reproduces these. See README.md.
#
